<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">John VI of Portugal</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1767">1767</a>–<a href="/years/1826">1826</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as João VI · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-braganza">House of Braganza</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">John VI was regent of Portugal from 1799 and king from 1816 to 1826. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> invaded in 1807 he moved the court to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a>, opened its ports and in 1815 raised it to a kingdom within the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-kingdom-of-portugal-brazil-and-the-algarves">United Kingdom of Portugal, Brazil and the Algarves</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liberal-revolution-of-1820">Liberal Revolution</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/porto">Porto</a> forced his return to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lisbon">Lisbon</a> in 1821, and his son Pedro declared Brazil's independence in 1822.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/john-vi-of-portugal.jpg" alt="John VI of Portugal" width="320" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Albert Gregorius · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Retrato_de_D._Joao_VI_-_Gregorius,_Albertus_Jacob_Frans.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>John was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lisbon">Lisbon</a> on 13 May 1767, the second son of Maria, Princess of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a>, later Queen Maria I, and Infante Peter, her paternal uncle, who became King Peter III. He grew up in the shadow of his elder brother José, and little is known of the substance of his education beyond instruction in religion, law, French and etiquette. In 1785 he married Carlota Joaquina of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, then ten years old, with a papal dispensation, since they were second cousins; the marriage was consummated five years later. After José died of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/smallpox">smallpox</a> in 1788, John became heir to the throne.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>As his mother's mental illness grew, John took control of the country, though he rejected a formal regency, and from 1799 he was prince regent. He tried to gain time against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a>'s pressure, refusing to obey the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/continental-system">Continental System</a> while a secret treaty with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> promised help if the royal family had to flee. In October 1807 news came that a French army was approaching, and a British squadron arrived at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lisbon">Lisbon</a> on 16 November. John chose British protection and sailed for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a>, reaching <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salvador">Salvador</a> on 22 January 1808.</p><p>In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a> his first major act was the decree opening the ports to friendly nations, from which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> gained most. He founded the Imprensa Régia, the Botanical Garden and the National Library of Brazil, along with schools, academies and a Royal Museum. He succeeded his mother on 20 March 1816 and was acclaimed on 6 February 1818. Carlota Joaquina repeatedly plotted for a monarchy of her own, and John's involvement in Spanish colonial politics led to the capture of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montevideo">Montevideo</a> in 1817 and the annexation of Cisplatina Province in 1821.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>The royal ships reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lisbon">Lisbon</a> on 3 July, and John swore loyalty to a new constitution on 1 October 1822, surrendering various prerogatives. Carlota Joaquina refused and lost her political rights. Meanwhile his son Pedro, who stayed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a>, proclaimed independence on 7 September 1822 and took the title of emperor. Tradition holds that John had told him to put the crown on his own head before an adventurer took it. His other son Miguel led a rebellion that sought to depose him. John was recognised as titular emperor of Brazil from 15 November 1825 and died on 10 March 1826.</p><p>Contemporaries thought him kind and benevolent, but later generations caricatured him, and historians have in recent decades restored his reputation as a king who balanced competing interests. Many consider him a mastermind of the modern Brazilian state.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “John VI of Portugal” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Brazilian independence came in 1822 under Pedro I, the son of King <b>João VI</b>, who refused the Lisbon Cortes’ attempts to restore Brazil’s colonial subordination.</blockquote><a href="/empire/portuguese-empire">Portuguese Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>On 16 December 1815 the <b>Prince Regent John</b> raised Brazil to a kingdom within the United Kingdom of Portugal, Brazil and the Algarves, ending colonial status for the State of Brazil.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-brazil">Kingdom of Brazil</a></li><li><blockquote>On <b>John VI</b>'s death in 1826 his heir Pedro, emperor of Brazil, granted Portugal a constitutional Charter and abdicated for his daughter Maria II, naming his brother Miguel regent.</blockquote><a href="/state/miguelist-portugal">Miguelist Portugal</a></li><li><blockquote>On 16 December 1815 the <b>Prince Regent John</b> raised Brazil from colony to the Kingdom of Brazil, uniting it with the Kingdom of Portugal and the Kingdom of the Algarve.</blockquote><a href="/state/united-kingdom-of-portugal-brazil-and-the-algarves">United Kingdom of Portugal, Brazil and the Algarves</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lisbon">Lisbon</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/portugal">Portugal</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lisbon">Lisbon</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/portugal">Portugal</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=38.71,-9.14&amp;year=1805" aria-label="Lisbon on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4323 775 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of John VI of Portugal"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4773 1045h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4773 1045h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=38.71,-9.14&amp;year=1805">What was here in 1805? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
